<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>Types of FMLA Violations</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Riley Chen (RC), Employment &amp; Civil Rights Practice Lead. Updated May 2026.</em></p>
  <h2>Interference with FMLA rights</h2><p>Refusing to approve a qualifying FMLA request, discouraging employees from taking leave, failing to designate qualifying leave as FMLA, counting FMLA absences in attendance or points systems, or failing to maintain health benefits during leave. 29 U.S.C. &sect; 2615(a)(1).</p>
  <h2>Retaliation / discrimination</h2><p>Taking adverse action against an employee because they took or requested FMLA leave: termination, demotion, negative performance review, reduction in hours, failure to promote. The causation standard is &ldquo;but-for&rdquo; in the Sixth Circuit and others, or motivating factor in some circuits. Close temporal proximity between leave and adverse action supports an inference of retaliation.</p>
  <h2>Notice and paperwork violations</h2><p>Employer obligations under the FMLA include providing timely notice of FMLA eligibility, rights, and responsibilities. Failure to provide the required notices (WH-381, WH-382) can be an independent violation and may estop the employer from denying leave on technical grounds.</p>
  <h2>Miscounting or misdesignating leave</h2><p>Counting leave taken for FMLA-qualifying reasons against an employee&rsquo;s regular attendance or PTO balance (when the employer is not permitted to substitute PTO), or failing to properly designate qualifying leave as FMLA (which prevents the leave from counting toward the 12-week annual cap).</p>
  <h2>Where to go from here</h2>
  <p>See <a href="/guides/how-fmla-damages-work">how FMLA damages work</a> and <a href="/guides/what-to-do-after-fmla-violation">what to do after a violation</a>.</p>
